5,"Bisorio language resources | Joshua ProjectNete-Bisorio [Newguineaworld]Bisorio Language (BIR) – L1 & L2 Speakers, Status, Map ...Bisorio [Newguineaworld]",https://joshuaproject.net/languages/bir,"Listing of people groups speaking Bisorio. Bisorio dialects and alternate names. Bible and ministry resource availability. Nete-Bisorio is comprised of two closely related languages, Nete and Bisorio, spoken from just north of the Lagaip River in far northwest of Papua New Guinea's Enga Province to the southern part of the Sepik Hills region in East Sepik province. Bisorio is an endangered indigenous language of Papua New Guinea. It belongs to the Trans-New Guinea language family. Direct evidence is lacking, but the language is thought to be used as a first language by a decreasing number of young people. It is not known to be taught in schools. Bisorio … Korosameri River in the the Sepik Hils region of Papua New Guinea's East Sepik Province.",Bisorio,Trans-New Guinea
